-**Status:** Accepted — amended in part by [ADR 036](036-approved-answer-lands-in-short-term-memory.md): §1 short-term memory is the current issue's folder only, and every lookup names its issue; §5 an approved researched answer is written to the issue's short-term memory, never straight to Garura or Phoenix memory, and moving it up is a promotion, its own memory update a person approves outside the work, while a person's own answer to a question goes to Garura memory; §7 the review tool's third section is Memory and ADRs, where every memory review and ADR review happens, on the Catch up cards (and with it §9's line on ADR 032 §3); and Negative / Risks, "Not decided here": its first two questions are settled — learned checkpoint answers are not memory, and a play's own working files stay direct (ADR 011); and amended in part by [ADR 037](037-agent-decisions-keep-a-trace-and-the-review-tool-has-five-sections.md): §6 "Every decision names its memory": a decision keeps a trace — the steps the agent took, in order, each with what it looked for, what it found, and the memory lines that said so (a tier, a file, the lines and a fingerprint of that text), kept with the exact text, the version of memory it was read from and up to three lines before and after — not one source ("the tier, the file and the lines it rests on"); a check reads every step's source again, names the step whose source changed or is gone, and flags a decision with no trace; §7 "A memory review" (a Memory view, made Memory and ADRs by ADR 036): the review tool's sections are Intents, Product, ADRs, Memory and Agent decisions, and which memory each decision came from is shown in Agent decisions, as its trace (and with it §9's line on ADR 032 §3); and amended in part by [ADR 038](038-install-brings-no-memory.md): §2 "Phoenix memory has one home; a project pulls the subset it needs": its line "At install, a project pulls a starting set: Garura's rulebook and the memory guide (part 8)" — the install pulls nothing; Phoenix memory reaches a machine the first time the memory tool needs it, downloaded whole from the source its settings name (today a git repository, later a server address) to one place on the machine (default ~/.garura/phoenix-memory), never waiting on a password and stopping after a time limit, and a failed download refuses the lookup with Phoenix memory named; a project's own Garura memory is set up after the install by a new play, `initialize`. The rest of §2 stands (its own git repository, pulling more on need, the list of what was pulled). §3: the line in Kapil's quote "installation is key so that it has some part of LTM build" — Garura memory is built by `initialize`, not the install; until it runs, lookups go up to Phoenix memory; the lookup order and "never blocks a play" stand. §4: "The tool sits outside Garura" — the tool is installed in each project at .garura/tools/memory/ with its settings beside it; agents still call the tool, never a path, so a move to Griffin still changes the tool only. §8 "Every install carries the memory system": the install lays the tool, its settings and the memory guide, brings no memory, and checks that the tool runs and reaches short-term and Garura memory; it does not fail when Phoenix memory is not on the machine yet — it reports it, and notes when the source does not answer. Alternatives Considered: the line "A project pulls nothing at install; every piece on first need" (lost to "a starting set at install") is now the choice for the install; and amended in part by [ADR 039](039-memory-writes-go-to-short-term-memory-plays-read-no-memory.md); and amended in part by [ADR 050](050-the-retired-intent-epic-pipeline-is-deleted.md): §10: of the seven retired agents, feature-steward, epic-expectation-crafter, product-keeper and market-analyst are deleted; the other three are left as they are
